<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  A TEMPLATE, not a loadable job. Every {ROOT} below is a placeholder.

  Render it with the project folder written in, and it becomes a plist launchd
  will accept. From the project folder:

    sed "s|{ROOT}|$PWD|g" config/launchd/templates/deadman.plist.tmpl \
      > ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.mtalib.agentic-trading.deadman.plist
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) \
      ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.mtalib.agentic-trading.deadman.plist

  WHY THIS ONE IS NOT GENERATED LIKE THE OTHER SIX. The six live jobs are written
  by scripts/gen_launchd.py from config/launchd/templates/*.template, and
  tests/test_paths.py checks that every generated file matches its template and
  that each job has exactly the number of wake ups it is meant to have. The dead
  man's handle is not switched on yet and has never run against a real account,
  so it is deliberately kept out of that set: adding it would put a job nobody
  has watched yet into the same list as the ones that are ready. When Mo decides
  to arm it, the move is small and worth doing properly:

    1. write config/launchd/templates/deadman.template in gen_launchd's own
       format, with the program, environment and comment from this file, and the
       schedule line reading exactly:
         every 5 minutes from 09:30 to 16:00 on weekdays
    2. add "deadman": 395 to EXPECTED_WAKE_UPS in tests/test_paths.py
    3. run scripts/gen_launchd.py to write the plist, then run it again with the
       install flag to load it

  WHAT IT DOES. Every five minutes from 09:30 to 16:00, Monday to Friday, it runs
  agent/deadman.py in its armed form. That checks whether the trading loop has
  gone quiet for more than fifteen minutes while the market is open and while a
  book still holds a position or has an order working. If all three are true it
  tells Mo and pulls the kill switch. If any one of them is false it does
  nothing, and in the healthy case it does not even speak to IB Gateway.

  IT CAN TRADE. This is the only launchd job in the project that can. It cannot
  open anything: the only orders it can cause are closing orders from
  agent/kill_switch.py, and only on an account whose id starts with DU. A live
  account would need AGENTIC_TRADING_KILL_LIVE set to yes in the environment
  block below, which is deliberately not there.

  Take the third argument out of ProgramArguments and it becomes a dry run: it
  still decides and still writes to the log, and it sends nothing and trades
  nothing. That is the way to watch it for a week before trusting it.

  launchd works in this Mac's own local time. There is no setting in here that
  pins a time zone, so the times below are this Mac's clock. The market keeps New
  York hours whatever the Mac thinks. Keep the Mac on Eastern, or change every
  time in here.

  NOT LOADED by writing this file. Loading is the separate, deliberate step
  written out above.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.mtalib.agentic-trading.deadman</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
      <string>{ROOT}/venv312/bin/python</string>
      <string>{ROOT}/agent/deadman.py</string>
      <string>--really</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>{ROOT}</string>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>{ROOT}/output/launchd_deadman.out.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>{ROOT}/output/launchd_deadman.err.log</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
      <key>AGENTIC_TRADING_ROOT</key><string>{ROOT}</string>
      <key>PATH</key><string>/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin</string>
      <key>TZ</key><string>America/New_York</string>
    </dict>

    <!-- A run that overruns must never have a second one land on top of it. The
         kill switch waits up to thirty seconds for the account to go flat, so
         two minutes is plenty and still well inside the five minute gap. -->
    <key>AbandonProcessGroup</key>
    <false/>
    <key>ExitTimeOut</key>
    <integer>120</integer>

    <!-- Do not fire the moment the job is loaded, only on the times below. A job
         that fired at load would judge a heartbeat from whenever the machine was
         last used, and at nine in the evening that is not a fault. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <!-- 395 wake ups a week: 79 a day, 09:30 to 16:00 every five minutes,
         Monday to Friday. -->
    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <array>
